    "summary": "Heavy fighting broke out in Baidoa, Somalia, between federal government troops and forces loyal to former president Mohamed Abdullahi Farmajo, also known as Lafta-gareen. The violence has raised concerns about stability in the South West state.",
    "content": "At least several people were killed and others wounded in Baidoa on [date] as clashes erupted between Somali federal forces and troops aligned with former President Mohamed Abdullahi Farmajo, widely referred to as Lafta-gareen. The confrontation occurred in the city, which serves as the administrative capital of the South West state, and involved heavy weaponry, according to local sources. The fighting marks a significant escalation in tensions between the federal government led by President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud and the political faction loyal to his predecessor.\n\nThe roots of the current instability trace back to a prolonged political dispute over electoral reforms and the federal government's handling of regional security. Since losing the 2022 presidential election, Farmajo has maintained a separate power base in parts of the country, particularly in the Gedo region and areas of Bay and Bakool, where his loyalists control key military units. Efforts to integrate these forces into a unified national army have repeatedly stalled, creating a decentralized and fragile security environment.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, who closely monitor homeland affairs and often remit funds to family members, the renewed violence in Baidoa is deeply concerning. Many from the Bay and Bakool regions have relatives who have already been displaced by earlier conflicts, including the 2020-2021 political crisis that led to a breakdown in security. The latest clashes risk triggering another wave of internal displacement, potentially exacerbating humanitarian needs in a region already grappling with drought and food insecurity.\n\nThe federal government has called for calm and urged both sides to return to dialogue, but no official statement confirming the details of the clashes was immediately available. Local elders have appealed for a ceasefire, fearing that the violence could spread to other districts and further undermine the fragile peace established in the area. The international community, including the African Union Transition Mission in Somalia (ATMIS), has monitored the situation, though no intervention has been confirmed.\n\nAs the situation remains fluid, diaspora communities are urged to follow verified updates from trusted sources. The long-term resolution of political rivalries will be critical to preventing further fragmentation of the Somali security forces and ensuring stability in the run-up to planned elections in 2026.\n\nRead the original report from Horseed Media here: https://horseedmedia.net/ciidamo-taabacsan-lafta-gareen-oo-weerar-ku-qaaday-magaalada-baydhabo/424883/",
