    "content": "In a significant milestone for Somalia's security sector, the national army held its largest graduation ceremony since the collapse of the central government in 1991. Approximately 3,000 soldiers completed a full year of basic military training and officially passed out on Sunday in the central Galgadud region. President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud personally attended the event, underscoring the government's commitment to rebuilding the armed forces.\n\nThe training program, funded by Saudi Arabia, provided recruits from across the country with instruction in military skills, discipline, military law, and combat tactics. This comprehensive curriculum is designed to prepare the new soldiers for the challenges of securing the nation and combating insurgent groups.\n\nThe graduation comes amid ongoing efforts to stabilize Somalia and transfer security responsibilities from international partners to national forces. The large-scale recruitment and training initiative reflects a strategic push to enhance the country's defense capabilities and reduce reliance on external military support.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this development signals a hopeful trajectory toward greater domestic security and stability, which could encourage investment and engagement with the homeland. The increased number of trained troops may also contribute to improved conditions for diaspora members visiting or conducting business in Somalia, as a stronger national army is expected to bolster law and order.\n\nRead the original report from Somalia Guardian here: https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/somalia-army-graduates-3000-troops-largest-class-since-1991/",
