    "title": "NISA conducts targeted operation in Hiiraan, kills senior militia leaders",
    "original_title": "NISA oo Hawlgal Qorshaysan ka Fulisay Tardo",
    "slug": "nisa-conducts-targeted-operation-in-hiiraan-kills-senior-militia-leaders",
    "summary": "Somalia's National Intelligence and Security Agency (NISA) carried out a coordinated operation in the Tardo area of Hiiraan region, killing three senior commanders and five militia fighters, and wounding four others. The operation, which involved international partners, targeted leaders responsible for attacks and extortion in the region.",
    "content": "Mogadishu – Somalia’s National Intelligence and Security Agency (NISA) announced on Sunday that its forces, in coordination with international partners, executed a planned operation in the Tardo area of the Hiiraan region. According to an official statement, the operation resulted in the deaths of three senior commanders and five militia members, while four other fighters were wounded.\n\nThe killed commanders were identified as Sicid Xuriye, who was reportedly responsible for the eastern Hiiraan sector, Xamse Bakool, the head of militias operating in Hiiraan, and Cusmaan, described as a senior leader within the group. NISA did not provide further details on the exact roles of the deceased, but noted that the operation was part of ongoing efforts to dismantle armed groups that have destabilized the region.\n\nHiiraan, located in central Somalia, has seen frequent clashes between government-aligned forces and al-Shabaab-linked militias. The area has also been a hotspot for militia activity that has disrupted trade and displaced civilians. The Tardo operation is one of several recent military actions aimed at reducing the influence of such groups along key supply routes in the region.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, these developments are significant because security stability in Hiiraan directly affects the safety of relatives and the flow of remittances. Many Somalis abroad regularly send funds to family members in rural areas, and insecurity often hampers access to banking and aid. Reports of successful military operations provide some reassurance, but diaspora communities remain cautious as threats persist.\n\nThe international community, including African Union forces and other partners, has supported Somali security agencies in building capacity for targeted operations. While these efforts have produced results, analysts note that long-term stability requires addressing governance and economic grievances that fuel recruitment by armed groups. NISA has pledged to continue operations against militia networks in the central regions.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/08/16/nisa-oo-hawlgal-qorshaysan-ka-fulisay-tardo/",
